[[File:Celtic FC trophy case.JPG|thumb|350px|A trophy case in the Celtic Park trophy room|alt=Photo of trophy case within the Celtic Park trophy room, several of the trophies draped in green and white coloured ribbons]]
[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic Football Club]] are a Scottish professional [[association football]] club based in [[Glasgow]]. They have played at their home ground, [[Celtic Park]], since 1892. Celtic were founding members of the [[Scottish Football League]] in 1890, and the [[Scottish Premier League]] in 1998 as well as the [[Scottish Professional Football League]] in 2013.

The list encompasses the major honours won by Celtic, records set by the club, their managers and their players. The player records section includes details of the club's leading goalscorers and those who have made most appearances in first-team competitions. It also records notable achievements by Celtic players on the international stage, and the highest transfer fees paid and received by the club. Attendance records at Celtic Park, and also at [[Hampden Park]] which has on occasion been used for home games, are also included.

Celtic have won 55 [[List of top-division football clubs in UEFA countries|top-flight]] titles, and hold the record for most [[Scottish Cup]] wins with 43. The club's record appearance maker is [[Billy McNeill]], who made 822 appearances between 1957 and 1975. [[Jimmy McGrory]] is the club's record goalscorer, scoring 522 goals during his Celtic career.

''All figures are correct as of 22 October 2026''
{{TOClimit|limit=3}}

==Honours==
Celtic's first ever silverware was won in 1889 when they defeated [[Cowlairs F.C.|Cowlairs]] 6–1 in the final of the [[Glasgow North Eastern Cup|North-Eastern Cup]].<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/> A year later they won the [[Glasgow Cup]], before winning their first major national honour in 1892 by defeating [[Queen's Park F.C.|Queen's Park]] 5–1 in the final of the [[Scottish Cup]].<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/> Celtic won their [[1892–93 Scottish Football League|first league title]] in [[1892–93 in Scottish football|1892–93]].<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/> In [[1906–07 Celtic F.C. season|1906–07]] Celtic became the first club to win the [[Double (association football)|league and cup double]] in Scotland,<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/> a feat they have now accomplished on 14 occasions. They won their first [[Treble (association football)#Domestic trebles|domestic treble]] in [[1966–67 Celtic F.C. season|1966–67]], the same season they became the first British club to win the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] with their 2–1 victory over [[Inter Milan]] in the final.<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/> Celtic's most recent success was their win in the [[2024–25 Scottish Premiership]]. Celtic have won a total of 122 major trophies.<ref>{{cite web |title=The 30 most successful football clubs in the world - based on total trophies won |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/30-most-successful-football-clubs-19428893 |website=www.mirror.co.uk|date=3 August 2020 }}</ref>

In all, Celtic have won the [[Scottish Premiership|Scottish League Championship]] a record 56 times, the [[Scottish Cup]] a record 43 times, the [[Scottish League Cup]] 22 times and the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] once. They have completed a world-record, eight domestic trebles, including an unprecedented quadruple treble between the [[2016–17 Celtic F.C. season|2016–17]] and [[2019–20 Celtic F.C. season|2019–20]] seasons.<ref name="Number of trebles">{{cite news|url=https://www.rsssf.org/miscellaneous/doublerec.html|publisher=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation|title=Doing the Double {{!}} Domestic Trebles|date=25 February 2021}}</ref>

