== Managerial records ==
{{further|List of Celtic F.C. managers}}

*First manager: [[Willie Maley]], from 1897 to 1940.<ref name="maley1">{{cite news|last=Cuddihy|first=Paul|title=Ronny Deila is Celtic's No.17|url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/6009|accessdate=15 September 2015|work=Celtic FC|date=6 June 2014}}</ref><ref name="maley">{{cite web|title=If You Know The History: 2nd April: Willie Maley died 1958|url=https://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2012/04/02/if-you-know-the-history-2nd-april-willie-maley-died-1958/|website=Jim Craig Celtic Supporter's Club|accessdate=18 September 2015|date=2 April 2012}}</ref>
*Longest-serving manager by time: [[Willie Maley]], 42 years and 9 months (April 1897 to 1 January 1940).<ref name="maley1" /><ref name="maley" />
*Shortest-serving manager by time: [[Wilfried Nancy]], 33 days (3 December 2025 to 5 January 2026).<ref>{{Cite news |last=Currie|first=David|date=2026-05-14 |title=Nancy rues lack of time after 'beautiful' 33-day Celtic spell |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c152l12ge19o |access-date=2026-06-06 |work=BBC|language=en-US}}</ref>
*Shortest-serving manager by matches: [[Wilfried Nancy]], 8 matches.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Currie|first=David|date=2026-05-14 |title=Nancy rues lack of time after 'beautiful' 33-day Celtic spell |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c152l12ge19o |access-date=2026-06-06 |work=BBC|language=en-US}}</ref>

==Club records==
===Matches===
====Firsts====
*First match: vs. [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], Friendly, Won 5–2, [[Celtic Park]] ([[Glasgow]]), (H), 28 May 1888<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/>
*First Scottish Cup match: vs. [[Shettleston]], Won 5–1, [[Celtic Park]] ([[Glasgow]]), (H), 1 September 1888<ref>{{cite web|title=Scottish Cup|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=105&competition=2&update=Update|website=FitbaStats|accessdate=30 March 2016}}</ref>
*First League match: vs. [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Heart of Midlothian]], Won 5–0, [[Tynecastle Stadium]] ([[Edinburgh]]), (A), 23 August 1890<ref>{{cite web|title=Results for Season 1890/1891 in Scottish League|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=103&competition=1&update=Update|website=FitbaStats|accessdate=2 October 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=The History of Tynecastle Stadium|url=http://www.heartsfc.co.uk/pages/historytynecastlestadium|website=Hearts FC|accessdate=2 October 2016}}</ref>
*First League Cup match: vs. [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]], Lost 2–4, [[Easter Road]] ([[Edinburgh]]), (H), 21 September 1946<ref>{{cite web|title=Competition History|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/comphist_comp.php|website=FitbaStats|accessdate=2 October 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Results for Season 1946/1947 in League Cup|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=66&competition=3&update=Update|website=FitbaStats}}</ref>
*First European match: vs. [[Valencia]], Lost 2–4, [[Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]], [[Mestalla Stadium|Mestalla]] ([[Spain]]), (A), 26 September 1962<ref>{{cite web|last=Craig |first=Jim |url=http://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2011/10/23/24th-october-1962-celtic-2-valencia-2-fairs-cup/ |title=24th October 1962 Celtic 2 Valencia 2 Fairs Cup &#124; Jim Craig Celtic Supporter's Club |work=Jim Craig Celtic Supporter's Club |date=23 October 2011 |accessdate=5 August 2013}}</ref>

===Wins===
*Record win: 11–0 (against [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]], 26 October 1895)<ref name="fsrecs">{{cite web|title=Alltime Club Records|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/club_records_overall.php|website=Fitbastats|accessdate=29 July 2015}}</ref>
*Record League win: 11–0 (against [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]], 26 October 1895)<ref name="fsrecs" />
*Record away win: 9–0 (against [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]], 28 August 2022)<ref name="auto">{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/62621464|title=Celtic hammer United in record away league win|work=BBC Sport }}</ref>
*Record away League win: 9–0 (against [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]], 28 August 2022)<ref name="auto"/>
*Record Scottish Cup win: 8–0 (against Cowlairs, 22 September 1888)<ref name="fsrecs" />
*Record League Cup win: 10–0 (against [[Hamilton Academical F.C.|Hamilton Academical]], 11 September 1968)<ref name="fsrecs" />
*Record European win: 9–0 (against KPV Kokkola, 16 September 1970)<ref>{{cite book|last=McGuirk|first=Brian|title=Celtic FC - the Ireland Connection|date=April 2009|publisher=Black and White Publishing|isbn=978-1845022488|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ah0jAwAAQBAJ&q=9-0+kpv+kokkola+%28Finland%29%2C+1970.e&pg=PT155|accessdate=28 September 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic F.C. 9 KPV Kokkola 0 |url=http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7071/kpv/kokkola7071h.htm |website=Celtic Programmes Online |accessdate=3 September 2015 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140825094654/http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7071/kpv/kokkola7071h.htm |archivedate=25 August 2014 }}</ref>

====Defeats====
*Record defeat: 0–8 (against [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]], 30 April 1937)<ref name="fsrecs" />
*Record League defeat: 0–8 (against [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]], 30 April 1937)<ref name="fsrecs" />
*Record Scottish Cup defeat:<ref name="sccuprecs">{{cite web|title=Scottish Cup Records|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/club_records_filtered.php?opposition=0&from=105&to=145&competition=2&update=Update|website=FitbaStats|accessdate=27 August 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=0-4 Vs Rangers in Scottish Cup (14/04/1928) |url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/game.php?gameid=76627 |website=FitbaStats |accessdate=6 September 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=0-4 Vs St Mirren in Scottish Cup (04/04/1959) |url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/game.php?gameid=28366 |website=FitbaStats |accessdate=6 September 2018}}</ref>
** 0–4 (against [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], 14 April 1928)
** 0–4 (against [[St. Mirren F.C.|St. Mirren]], 4 April 1959)
*Record League Cup defeat:
** 2–6 (against [[Clyde F.C.|Clyde]], 23 March 1946)<ref name="lgcuprecs">{{cite web|title=League Cup Records|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/club_records_filtered.php?opposition=0&from=105&to=145&competition=3&update=Update|website=FitbaStats|accessdate=27 August 2015|archive-date=24 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924013441/http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/club_records_filtered.php?opposition=0&from=105&to=145&competition=3&update=Update|url-status=dead}}</ref>
** 0–4 (against Rangers, 31 August 1955)<ref name="lgcuprecs" />
*Record European defeat: 0–7 (against [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], 13 September 2016)<ref name="English">{{cite news|last=English|first=Tom|title=Barcelona 7 - 0 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/37331224|accessdate=14 September 2016|work=BBC Sport|date=13 September 2016}}</ref>

===Goals===
*Most League goals scored in a season: 116 goals in the 1915–16 season<ref name="fsrecs" />
*Most goals scored in all competitions in a season: 196 goals in the 1966–67 season<ref name="fsrecs" />

===Points===
*Most points in a season (3 points per win): 106 (during the [[2016–17 in Scottish football|2016–17 season]]).<ref>{{cite book|last=McGurk|first=Brian|title=Celtic F.C. - the Ireland Connection|date=2009|publisher=Black & White Publishing Ltd|isbn=9781845022488|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ah0jAwAAQBAJ&q=celtic+most+points+in+a+season+103&pg=PT154|chapter=Club records}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Campbell|first=Andy|title=Celtic 2 - 0 Hearts|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39911911|accessdate=22 May 2017|work=BBC Sport|date=21 May 2017}}</ref>
*Most points in a season (2 points per win): 72 (during the [[1987–88 in Scottish football|1987–88 season]]).<ref name="lgpts">{{cite book|last=Wilson|first=Brian|title=A Century with Honour|date=1988|publisher=Willow Books|isbn=9780002182300|pages=192–194}}</ref>
*Fewest points in a season (2 points per win):
**21 (during the 1896–97 season over 18 games).<ref name="lgpts" />
**25 (during the 1947–48 season over 30 games).<ref name="lgpts" />

===Attendances===
*Record attendance: 147,365 (against [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]], won 2–1, Hampden Park (N), 24 April 1937). (A record for a Football match in Europe)<ref name="CFCBriefHistory"/>
*Record Scottish League home attendance: 83,500 (against [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], won 3–0, Celtic Park (H), 1 January 1938).{{refn|group=nb|Newspaper reports at the time indicate that the officially returned attendance was given as 83,500, with an estimated further 10,000 supporters locked out of the ground for safety reasons. However, the ground's capacity was gauged at the time as being around 88,000 and several subsequent sources (including the club's official website) have since revised the attendance up to 92,000.}}<ref>{{cite news|title=Happy New Year for Celtic and Queens Park|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=qz9AAAAAIBAJ&pg=3781%2C312161|accessdate=14 November 2016|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=3 January 1938|page=16}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic in all time records|url=http://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=512&teamTabs=records|website=Soccerbase|accessdate=28 September 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Hannan|first1=Martin|title=Hail! Hail!: Classic Celtic Old Firm Clashes|date=2012|publisher=Mainstream Publishing|isbn=9781780577128|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=MKBefdszfwMC&q=celtic+92000+83500&pg=PT70|accessdate=14 November 2016}}</ref>
*Record European match attendance: 133,961 (against [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]], won 2–1, [[Hampden Park]] (H), 15 April 1970). (A record for a match in UEFA European competition)<ref name="ec6970">{{cite web|title=Season 1969-70|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929083252/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=29 September 2007|work=European Cup History|accessdate=6 October 2013}}</ref>

==Other records and statistics==
* World record for total number of goals scored in a season (competitive games only): 196 (1966–67 season)<ref>{{cite web|title=World Record and Statistics|url=http://xtrahistory.blogspot.co.uk/2014/04/Record-Statistics.html|work=World Football Historic Center|accessdate=17 June 2014}}</ref>
* UK record for an unbeaten run in professional football: 69 games (60 won; 9 drawn) 16 May 2016 to 17 December 2017.<ref>{{cite news|title=Hearts 4–0 Celtic - champions' unbeaten domestic run ends|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/42331728|accessdate=17 December 2017|work=BBC Sport|date=17 December 2017}}</ref>
* SPL record for an unbeaten run of home matches (League, Europe, Scottish Cup, League Cup) (77), from 2001 to 2004<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/3079953/Football-fortresses-Jose-Mourinho-makes-it-100-not-out-Football.html |title=Football fortresses: Jose Mourinho makes it 100 not out |work=The Telegraph |accessdate=20 February 2017 |date=26 September 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Dons+see+off+confident+Celtic.-a0115670761 |title=Dons see off confident Celtic |work=The Journal |date=22 April 2004 |accessdate=20 February 2017}}</ref>
* 14 consecutive League Cup final appearances, from 1964–65 season to 1977–78 inclusive,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.188-football.co.uk/content/feedstory/scottish-league-cup-history |title=Scottish League Cup&nbsp;– History |work=188 Football |accessdate=18 May 2009 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110902205348/http://www.188-football.co.uk/content/feedstory/scottish-league-cup-history |archivedate=2 September 2011}}</ref> a world record for successive appearances in the final of a major football competition<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7879/season7879.htm |title=Celtic FC 1978/79 |work=Celtic Programmes Online |accessdate=23 December 2009 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130314003833/http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7879/season7879.htm |archivedate=14 March 2013}}</ref>
* Most successive Scottish Cup tie wins: 19 (22 January 2017 – 19 December 2020; none going to extra time nor penalties aside from the last match on 19 December 2020)<ref>{{cite web |title=Club Results, Result Streaks (Scottish Cup) |url=https://fitbastats.com/celtic/club_result_streaks.php?opposition=0&from=105&to=151&competition=2&venue=0&usefilter=0&usefilter=on&update=Update |website=FitbaStats |access-date=1 September 2021}}</ref>
* Most successive League Cup tie wins: 19 (19 August 1967 – 12 August 1969)<ref>{{cite web |title=Club Results, Result Streaks (League Cup) |url=https://fitbastats.com/celtic/club_result_streaks.php?opposition=0&from=105&to=151&competition=3&venue=0&usefilter=0&usefilter=on&update=Update |website=FitbaStats |access-date=1 September 2021}}</ref>
* Most goals scored in one Scottish top-flight league match by one player: 8 goals by Jimmy McGrory against [[Dunfermline Athletic F.C.|Dunfermline]] in 9–0 win on 14 January 1928<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/1759?item=1759 |title=Dixie hits Jags for six |work=Celtic FC |date=17 November 2011 |accessdate=19 November 2011}}</ref>
* Highest score in a domestic British cup final: Celtic 7–1 Rangers, [[Hampden in the sun|Scottish League Cup Final 1957]]<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/news/y=2009/m=2/news=old-firm-enduring-appeal-1023776.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150927174808/http://www.fifa.com/news/y=2009/m=2/news=old-firm-enduring-appeal-1023776.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=27 September 2015 |title=Celtic vs Rangers&nbsp;– Old Firm's enduring appeal |website=FIFA.com |accessdate=20 February 2017}}</ref>
* Fastest hat-trick in European Club Football&nbsp;– [[Mark Burchill]] vs [[Jeunesse Esch]] in 2000; 3 minutes (between 12th minute and 15th minute), a record at the time<ref name=WeeRed81>[http://www.eveningtimes.co.uk/81-fascinating-football-facts-all-from-the-wee-red-book-1.970916 81 fascinating football facts&nbsp;– all from the Wee Red Book] Evening Times, 3 July 2009</ref><ref name="eht">{{cite news |url=https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-64562738.html |title=O'Neill tells hat-trick hero Burch: You're not moving |work=Daily Record |date=25 August 2000 |archive-date=2012-10-25 |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121025051647/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-64562738.html |location=Glasgow, Scotland |url-status=usurped |access-date=1 January 2014}}</ref>
* Earliest SPL Championship won: won with 8 games remaining in [[2016–17 Celtic F.C. season|2017]] (equalling all-time Scottish top-flight record set by Rangers in [[1928–29 Scottish Division One|1929]])<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic's title triumph by numbers|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39330673|accessdate=4 April 2017|work=BBC Sport|date=2 April 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/competitions/premiership/celtic-are-earliest-scottish-title-champions-in-88-years-1-4410041 |title=Celtic are earliest Scottish title champions in 88 years |work=[[The Scotsman]] |date=2 April 2017 |accessdate=4 April 2017}}</ref>
* Biggest margin of victory in the SPL. 9–0 against Aberdeen, 6 November 2010<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.scotprem.com/content/default.asp?page=s47 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130524074137/http://www.scotprem.com/content/default.asp?page=s47 |title=Records |work=Scottish Premier League |archivedate=24 May 2013}}</ref>
* Celtic and [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] hold the record for the biggest transfer fee between two Scottish clubs. Celtic bought [[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]] from Hibernian on 16 May 2007 for £4.4m<ref>{{cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/6661737.stm |title=Brown completes switch to Celtic |work=BBC Sport |date=16 May 2007 |access-date=20 February 2017}}</ref>
* Most expensive transfer from Scottish football to another country, [[Kieran Tierney]] to [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], August 2019, for about £25 million.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49283667 |title=Kieran Tierney: Celtic defender completes £25m Arsenal move |website=BBC Sport |date=8 August 2019 |accessdate=8 August 2019}}</ref>
* First weekly club publication in the UK, ''The Celtic View''<ref>{{cite book|last1=Campbell|first1=Tom|last2=Potter|first2=David|title=Jock Stein: The Celtic Years|date=7 October 1999|publisher=Mainstream Publishing|isbn=1-84018-2415|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=1GBb9phYrywC&q=jack+mcginn+beaverbrook&pg=PA1944|accessdate=1 August 2014}}</ref>
* First European club to field a player from the Indian sub-continent, [[Mohammed Salim (footballer)|Mohammed Salim]]<ref name="Mohammed Salim at Celtic">{{cite news|url=http://www.scotsman.com/sport/barefooted_indian_who_left_calcutta_to_join_celtic_1_1151472|title=Barefooted Indian who left Calcutta to join Celtic|work=The Scotsman|date=12 December 2008|accessdate=10 April 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://the-shamrock.net/2013/12/18/salim/|title=SALIM! Celtic's First Asian Sensation|website=the-shamrock.net|date=18 December 2013|access-date=21 September 2021}}</ref>
* First British club to reach the final of the European Cup, and the only Scottish, and first British team to win the European Cup<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1966-67|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro67.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926230931/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro67.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=26 September 2007|work=European Cup History|accessdate=6 October 2013}}</ref>

==European statistics==
{{Main|Celtic F.C. in international football}}
* Most appearances: [[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]], 127<ref name="brbr1"/><ref name="brbr2" /><ref name="brbr3" />
* Most goals: [[Henrik Larsson]], 35<ref>{{cite web|title=Henrik Larsson|url=http://www.soccerbase.com/players/player.sd?player_id=11617|website=Soccerbase|accessdate=6 July 2015}}</ref>
* Biggest win: Celtic 9–0 KPV Kokkola, in the European Cup, 16 September 1970<ref>{{cite book|last=McGuirk|first=Brian|title=Celtic FC - the Ireland Connection|date=April 2009|publisher=Black and White Publishing|isbn=978-1-84502-248-8|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ah0jAwAAQBAJ&q=9-0+kpv+kokkola+%28Finland%29%2C+1970.e&pg=PT155|accessdate=28 September 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic F.C. 9 KPV Kokkola 0|url=http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7071/kpv/kokkola7071h.htm|website=Celtic Programmes Online|accessdate=3 September 2015 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140825094654/http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7071/kpv/kokkola7071h.htm |archivedate=25 August 2014}}</ref>
* Biggest defeat: [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] 7–0 Celtic in the UEFA Champions League, 13 September 2016<ref name="English"/>
* Highest home attendance (Hampden Park): 136,505, against Leeds United in the 1969–70 European Cup<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1969–70|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929083252/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=29 September 2007|website=European Cup History|accessdate=6 October 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Bloomfield|first=Craig|title=Top 10 biggest attendances in European Cup (Champions League) history – unbelievably huge crowds|url=http://talksport.com/football/top-10-biggest-attendances-european-cup-champions-league-history-unbelievably-huge-crowds?p=9|website=Talk Sport|accessdate=23 November 2016|date=13 September 2013}}</ref>{{refn|group=nb|The home leg of Celtic's 1970 [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] semi-final tie against [[Leeds United]] was switched from [[Celtic Park]] (which had a capacity at the time of around 80,000) to [[Hampden Park]] due to the expected high demand for tickets.}}
::::::::: (Celtic Park): 77,240, against Fiorentina in the 1969–70 European Cup<ref>{{cite web|title=3-0 Vs Fiorentina in European Champions Cup (04/03/1970)|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/game.php?gameid=76197|website=FitbaStats|accessdate=23 November 2016}}</ref>

